{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the ruler of Tripoli from 1149 to 1152 and later in 1153 and 1157. He was a member of the House of Toulouse and a descendant of the Alphege family. \n\n*Reign and Titles*\n\nRaymond III was the ruler of Tripoli from 1149 to 1152 and from 1153 to 1157. He was crowned King of Jerusalem in 1149 and was a member of the Crusader state known as the Kingdom of Jerusalem. However he never completed his coronation and he was never recognized as a legitimate king by the Holy Roman Empire. \n\n*Military Campaigns and Relations with Neighbors*\n\nRaymond III led several military campaigns against neighboring lands including the city of Sidon which he recaptured in 1151. He also fought against the Zengid dynasty in the Levant and their vassals in Jaffa. Raymond III formed an alliance with the Zengid ruler Nur ad-Din Zengi to counter the growing power of the Ayyubid dynasty in Egypt and Syria. \n\n*Assassination and Legacy*\n\nRaymond III was assassinated by the people of Tripoli in 1152. The reason behind the assassination is not known but it is believed to be due to his aggressive military campaigns and policies which were unpopular with the local population. After his death the ruler of Tripoli became Amalric I who was a member of the House of Ibelin.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0013606823049485683}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001284993631998077}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.19242680072784424}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.182837463100441e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.0003156163729727268}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -7.188061863416806e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.007730093784630299}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.2560997009277344}, {"token": " ruler", "logprob": -0.6396949887275696}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0002619877